\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9\ldblquote Heroes are often hard to find in American literature.\rdblquote  \par
\par
 \par
\par
In the texts I have studied, being The Grapes of Wrath and The Great Gatsby (hereafter GOW and Gatsby, respectively), there are frequently few heroes to be found. Instead, authors including Steinbeck and Fitzgerald seek to present realistic depictions of characters that leave little room for archetypal heroes. Further, Edith Wharton, in her work Ethan Frome, similarly does not focus on a heroic character and instead discusses human relationships from a grounded perspective. It is difficult to see the main characters of these works as heroes in their own right, and it is often other characters in these works that provide the moral centre. To an extent this can be attributed to ideas of what an American hero should be; in a deeply patriarchal time, this idea was shaped by figures such as the Emersonian ideal and the ideas of Transcendentalism. These notions focus more on self-reliance rather than some form of heroism, and this further does not give traditional literary heroes a place in the American literature of this period. \par
\par
 \par
\par
The protagonists of the works I have studied at this time are frequently not shown to be heroic. In Gatsby, the eponymous Gatsby is willing to shirk what can be seen as traditional moral standards in the eyes of pursuing his goal of finding a relationship with Daisy. His actions are at best morally dubious but could be conceived as malicious and intensely harmful to those around him. He is willing to break prohibition laws and bootleg alcohol and distribute it through drug stores as a way of procuring wealth. Not only is this deeply illegal, but it could be seen as amoral by the prevailing social order of the time. He uses this to gain obscene wealth as a way to try and impress and win back Daisy. He goes on to throw hedonistic parties nightly in the hopes she would \lquote wander into one of his parties some night\rquote . Not only is this an expensive and preposterous scheme it can also be seen as morally damaging and far from heroic. Not only does Gatsby lack directness, which could be seen as a heroic or traditional attribute he is also willing to create an environment for the city's elite to hedonistically denigrate themselves for his own selfish gain. He shows very little care for the rest of the people at his party, who all just show up uninvited and enjoy themselves. While this might be seen as generous, the motivation is wholly selfish, and it could be argued it actually damages the lives of the people attending through repeated consumption of damaging substances. Additionally, Gatsby befriends many criminal contacts, most notably Meyer Wolfsheim, who is noted to have \lquote fixed the World Series back in 1919\rquote . This demonstrates not only Wolfsheim\rquote s immense power and control over the general world, but it also demonstrates his lack of morality. For Gatsby to choose to surround himself with superficial partygoers and morally bankrupt gamblers must reflect his own lack of heroism. While Gatsby is not necessarily the protagonist of Gatsby, he must be understood as one of the most important characters. Gatsby becomes immensely wealthy and successful, but this cannot be necessarily correlated to moral righteousness or goodness. In GOW, Tom Joad also cannot be simply understood as the moral centre of the book or a hero. Tom murders someone before the start of the book, leading to a stint in prison. When Tom returns to his family, it is exclaimed to Tom by Grampa Joad that by committing the murder, \lquote He done just what I\rquote d do\rquote ; this shows a moral dealignment present with some members of the families. It also does not show remorse for the act committed, and this could be seen as evidence of a lack of heroism or moral presence. Further, near the end of the novel, Tom kills someone in revenge for the death of his close friend Jim Casy, and while this can perhaps be understood better than his last drunken fight, it is still a far from heroic action. The audience might disagree with this sharp \lquote eye for an eye\rquote  view of justice and thus find Tom to be unheroic. However, Tom is portrayed as wishing the best for his family and continuously acting in their interests despite some of his other moral failings. Tom, like Gatsby, is a character likely to be somewhat sympathised with by a reader but unlikely to be regarded as heroic due to his many more dubious actions. Similarly, the titular Ethan Frome can be seen as morally failing due to wishing to cheat on and leave his wife for a younger woman. This, however, can be well understood by the audience due to the efforts of Edith Wharton to show the emotional and physical isolation experienced by Frome. Again, Frome is a character with understandable motivations who readers simply cannot regard as heroic. However, when these characters are understood through the lens of individualism or the Emersonian ideal, it could be understood that as independent figures who frequently represent their own interests, they may be heroes in this sense. This clashes with a traditional moralistic view of heroism but represents more modern American concepts. Overall, the protagonists or central characters of these works are frequently shown to not be heroic and thus are hard to find. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Other characters in these works can provide a moral centre and thus could be seen as more heroic to some degree, but it varies between texts. In Gatsby this can be clearly understood as Nick Carraway, who serves as the narrator of the work. Nick could clearly be understood as more moral than Gatsby due to him not having direct ties to criminality and not perpetuating it. Additionally, Nick does not seek to pursue anyone who is already married in the way that Gatsby does; whether Gatsby is right to or not, he still tries and succeeds to commit adultery and damage a pre-existing marriage. To both readers of today and those contemporary with the text, this must be understood as damaging. Nick, however, does not directly engage in these actions and instead pursues the single Jordan Baker. However, Nick enables Gatsby by helping him to pursue Daisy (Nick\rquote s cousin) without much concern for the feelings of Tom Buchannan. Additionally, Nick benefits directly from Gatsby\rquote s wealth and criminality, frequently enjoying his parties and meeting figures such as Jordan or Myrtle\rquote s cousin. Additionally, Nick, rather than breaking from convention, falls heavily into the hedonistic party mindset of New York and Egg\rquote s elite. He eventually resents this, but instead of taking a stand against it, he falls into it heavily himself. He can be seen as more sympathetic than Gatsby by a reader but cannot be seen as heroic, as he is frequently just following others. Contrastingly, in GOW we get a much more clearly heroic figure in the preacher of Him, Casy. He acts as a moral centre and political figure and represents many of the beliefs of the author John Steinbeck. He espouses a clear spiritualist and leftist philosophy and selflessly acts throughout the novel. This can be seen in the Weedpatch camp, where he lets himself get arrested rather than Tom, as to not split up the family. His stint in prison would lead to him spreading his ideas and eventually beginning unionising in California; this shows a strong set of beliefs that do not bow or bend to institutional pressure. This strong belief system is something that Nick, for example, does not have, but Casy\rquote s beliefs never leave him. Additionally, Casy is willing to die for his beliefs, which is something deeply admirable to many. It renders him a Christlike figure willing to sacrifice for something he truly believes in. This increases his heroism greatly in the eyes of the reader and allows him to be similar to one of the most traditional literary heroes. Casy throughout the book is presented as someone who left his sins behind and has become wholly heroic. This sort of moral centre is not present in Ethan Frome, and this could perhaps be attributed to the very small scope of the work. Overall, most of these works do not feature a hero, but notably, GOW does in the form of Jim Casy. \par
\par
 \par
\par
American literature seeks to present real people rather than strict archetypes such as the traditional hero. This is highly notable in Gatsby,, where the majority of characters ust be conceived as morally grey or slightly morally dubious. The Buchannans re an exception who are frequently shown to have \lquote smashed up things and creatures and then retreated back into their money or their vast carelessness\rquote . Other characters such as Gatsby, Nick and Jordan all have transactive relationships and seek to do the best by themselves. This represents a highly individualistic viewpoint that could represent the Jeffersonian vision of Americans or Transcendentalism. The book and perhaps the wider moral compass of America at the time seemto forgive people of their crimes if they serve as a part of a path towards individual success. America as a melting pot and opportunity seems to extend this idea of rebirth to many of its citizens,, even those with criminal backgrounds. The characters are shown as people simply trying to succeed in life. The hedonistic individualism which is pervasive throughout the book prevents anyone from truly being a hero. This ties into the American belief that people have an \lquote unalienable right to life, liberty and the pursuit of happiness\rquote . The characters do not feel pressured to and thus do not seek moral purity,, as it is seen as acceptable for them to simply pursue happiness hedonistically. This further relates to the hedonistic tendencies of the lost generation in the period after WW1, who would act much like this. In GOW there is a hunt for survival at all costs; this leads to undoubtedly flawed behaviour. Characters have flaws, such as Uncle John\rquote s alcoholism. It is said by Pa when John goes to get drunk that \lquote A fella has to do what he has to do. Nobody\rquote  knows enough to tell\rquote  him.\rquote  And this shows a clear belief that people must do what they have to do to survive even if these actions are not pure. The characters in GOW are willing to abandon a heroic morality if it leads to further survival, as survival is the ultimate goal. The family unit keeps these people together despite their individual failings. Rather than celebrating the individual for not being a hero, instead we see many individuals brought together to create something greater. This closely aligns with Steinbeck\rquote s politics. From a Marxist viewpoint it makes sense that Casy, someone with awakened class consciousness, is truly seen as a hero. The rest are just people, proletariat in a bourgeois-dominated world. This is further true in Ethan Frome, where the characters are individuals struggling with their situations. Ethan\rquote s affair can be viewed sympathetically. Overall, the living situations of characters in American novels can force them to abandon their morals, stopping them from being heroes. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Overall, it is hard and almost impossible to find heroes in the works of American literature I have studied. While there are some heroes, rarely is it difficult to find truly morally pure characters, and this can be largely attributed to the situations characters are in. Additionally, the individualistic philosophy pervasive in American thinking dissuades people from being heroic or morally good. So it is unlikely to find heroes, but there are some rare exceptions, such as Jim Casy. In conclusion, heroes are rare, and this can be attributed to the philosophical ideals, settings and realism of American literature. \par
